Around 200 million years ago, some crocodile relatives were slim, long-legged hunters that ran on land. Others moved into the sea, swapping legs for flippers and growing tails like a fish. A few had blunt teeth for chewing plants.

Some were giants. *Sarcosuchus*, nicknamed "SuperCroc", lived in Africa about 110 million years ago and grew perhaps 9 to 11 metres long, about the length of a city bus. In North America, *Deinosuchus* grew nearly as big and probably ate dinosaurs that came to drink. Today's crocodilians, the crocodiles, alligators, caimans and gharials, are only one branch of this once crowded tree.

{{story:who-survived}}

There's a simple rule behind small animals' superpowers, called the square-cube law. The strength of a [[@muscles|muscle]] depends on how thick it is, its cross-section. But an animal's weight depends on its whole volume.

Make an animal twice as long, keeping the same shape, and its muscles become four times as strong. Its weight, though, goes up eight times. So bigger animals get relatively weaker. A beetle carries almost no weight of its own, so nearly all its muscle power is free for pushing.

{{story:muscles}}

अरबी सुलेख क़ुरान की नकल उतारने के इर्द-गिर्द पनपा। इस्लाम में पारंपरिक रूप से पवित्र जगहों पर इंसानों और जानवरों के चित्रों से बचा जाता है, इसलिए मस्जिदें अक्सर शब्दों से सजाई जाती हैं। सदियों में सुलेखकों ने [[@arabic-script|अरबी लिपि]] की कई शैलियाँ बनाईं, चौकोर कूफ़ी से लेकर बहती हुई सुलुस तक।

यह कला फ़ारसी और मुग़ल शासकों के साथ भारत आई, जिन्हें *नस्तालीक़* नाम की एक नज़ाकत भरी, लटकती-सी शैली बहुत पसंद थी। [[@taj-mahal|ताजमहल]] पर [[arch|मेहराबों]] के चारों ओर सफ़ेद संगमरमर में काले पत्थर की आयतें जड़ी हैं। सुलेखक अमानत ख़ाँ ने वहाँ अपना नाम भी लिखा, जो उस ज़माने के किसी कलाकार के लिए बहुत कम देखी जाने वाली बात थी।

{{story:fonts}}

In Japan, slurping hot [[@noodles-pasta|noodles]] is fine and even shows you like them. It also cools each mouthful on the way in. In Britain or the USA, children are often told to eat quietly with their mouths closed.

Plates send signals too. In many Indian and American homes, a clean plate tells the cook you enjoyed the meal. In parts of China, leaving a little food can show the host gave you more than enough. The same empty plate can mean "delicious" in one house and "I'm still hungry" in another.

{{story:japan-culture}}

मिस्र की एक प्राचीन चित्रकारी में एक मज़दूर एक मूर्ति ले जा रही स्लेज के आगे पानी डालता दिखता है। 2014 में वैज्ञानिकों ने इसकी वजह दिखाई: गीली रेत कहीं ज़्यादा सख़्त होती है, इसलिए स्लेज खींचने में लगभग आधा ही [[force|बल]] लगता है। सही मात्रा में पानी स्लेज और ज़मीन के बीच [[@friction|घर्षण]] को घटा देता है।

मज़दूर टुकड़ों को उठाने और खिसकाकर जगह पर बिठाने के लिए [[@lever-lift|लीवर]] का इस्तेमाल भी करते थे, और उन्हें आकार देने के लिए ताँबे की छेनियाँ और सख़्त पत्थर के हथौड़े। एक आम टुकड़े का वज़न लगभग 2.5 टन होता है, दो छोटी कारों जितना। ख़ूफ़ू के दफ़्न-कक्ष के ऊपर ग्रेनाइट की सिलें इससे कहीं भारी हैं, और वे नील नदी के ऊपरी हिस्से में लगभग 800 किलोमीटर दूर असवान से लाई गई थीं।

{{story:mummies}}

Trade is far older than countries as we know them. Thousands of years ago, the people of the Indus Valley traded beads and cotton with [[@mesopotamia|Mesopotamia]]. Silk, porcelain and paper moved west along the [[@silk-road|Silk Road]], while horses, glass and silver moved east.

{{story:silk-road}}

India's coasts were famous for [[@spices|spices]], especially [[@cardamom-pepper|black pepper]] from [[@kerala|Kerala]]. The search for a sea route to the spice markets drove Vasco da Gama to sail around Africa to Kozhikode in 1498.

Some of the world's favourite games began in India. [[@ludo|Ludo]] comes from pachisi, played on a cross-shaped cloth board with cowrie shells instead of dice. At Fatehpur Sikri, the city built by the emperor [[@akbar|Akbar]], a giant pachisi board is laid into a courtyard. Stories say he played with people as the pieces.

[[@snakes-ladders|Snakes and ladders]] comes from Gyan Chaupar, also called Moksha Patam. Players climbed ladders of virtues, such as generosity, and slid down snakes of vices, such as anger. The goal at the top was moksha, freedom from the cycle of rebirth. When the British took the game home in the 1800s, they kept the snakes and ladders and dropped most of the religion.

{{story:chess-india}}

Seeing something and moving in response usually takes a person about a fifth of a second. Your eyes must send a signal to your brain, your brain must decide, and your muscles must move.

Top players get around this with practice. They've watched thousands of strokes, so they recognise the start of a swing and its likely result. Studies of expert players show their eyes often jump ahead to where the ball will land rather than following it all the way. Their quick [[@reflexes|reflexes]] matter, but their ability to predict matters more.

{{story:reflexes}}

The Eras Tour ran from 2023 to 2024, with 149 shows on five continents. Each three-hour show travelled through all her musical "eras". Fans made and swapped friendship bracelets with song lyrics on them.

At her July 2023 concerts in Seattle, the dancing crowd shook the ground enough for scientists at the Pacific Northwest Seismic Network to record it. News reports called it the "[[@what-if-everyone-jumped|Swift Quake]]", and some compared it to a small [[@earthquakes|earthquake]] of about magnitude 2.3.

{{story:earthquakes}}

म्यूकस ज़्यादातर पानी है, जिसमें म्यूसिन नाम के चिपचिपे प्रोटीन मिले होते हैं, जो इसे लिसलिसा बनाते हैं। इसमें [[antibody|एंटीबॉडी]] और [[enzyme|एंज़ाइम]] भी होते हैं जो कीटाणुओं पर हमला करते हैं। ये तुम्हारे [[@immune-system|प्रतिरक्षा तंत्र]] का हिस्सा हैं।

इनमें से एक एंज़ाइम संयोग से मिला। 1921 में स्कॉटलैंड के वैज्ञानिक अलेक्ज़ेंडर फ़्लेमिंग को, जिन्होंने बाद में पेनिसिलिन खोजी, ज़ुकाम था। उन्होंने अपनी नाक का थोड़ा बलगम बैक्टीरिया की एक तश्तरी पर टपका दिया और देखा कि उसके पास के बैक्टीरिया मर गए। उन्होंने लाइसोज़ाइम खोज लिया था, एक कुदरती कीटाणु-नाशक, जो आँसुओं और लार में भी होता है।

{{story:air-pollution}}

Most animals on Earth wear an exoskeleton. Insects, spiders, centipedes and crustaceans together make up more than three-quarters of all known animal [[species|species]]. Their shells are made largely of a tough material called chitin, sometimes hardened with minerals.

There is a catch. An outer skeleton can't grow. To get bigger, the animal must moult, splitting its old casing and crawling out soft and helpless until a new one hardens. Exoskeletons also get very heavy as an animal gets bigger, which is one reason you'll never meet a beetle the size of a dog.

{{story:crabs}}

A normal bit is like a [[@light-switch|light switch]]: on or off. A qubit is more like a spinning coin that hasn't landed. While it spins, it carries a mix of heads and tails. When you look, it lands one way or the other.

The real power comes from [[interference|interference]]. Quantum states behave a bit like waves on a pond. Where two crests meet, they add up. Where a crest meets a dip, they cancel. A quantum program is designed so that paths leading to wrong answers cancel and paths leading to the right answer grow. When you finally measure, the right answer is the likely one.

{{story:binary-code}}

3 अगस्त 1492 को कोलंबस स्पेन के पालोस बंदरगाह से तीन जहाज़ों, नीन्या, पिंटा और सांता मारिया, और लगभग 90 आदमियों के साथ निकले। मुख्य जहाज़ सांता मारिया शायद लगभग 18 मीटर लंबा था, क्रिकेट की पिच से भी छोटा।

कैनरी द्वीपों पर रुकने के बाद वे एक महीने से ज़्यादा पश्चिम की ओर चलते रहे, और कहीं ज़मीन नहीं दिखी। नाविक बेचैन होने लगे। 12 अक्टूबर को निगरानी पर बैठे एक नाविक ने एक द्वीप देखा, जिसे ताइनो लोग गुआनाहानी कहते थे। कोलंबस ने इसका नाम सान साल्वाडोर रखा और इसे स्पेन का घोषित कर दिया।

{{story:chillies}}

Around the 1st century CE, the Satavahanas lost much of their western land to the Shakas, rulers whose ancestors came from Central Asia. Under Gautamiputra Satakarni, in the early 2nd century CE, they struck back.

He defeated the Shaka ruler Nahapana, and in a satisfying show of victory he had Nahapana's silver coins stamped again with his own name and symbols. Hoards of these over-struck coins have been found in Maharashtra. The Nashik inscription calls Gautamiputra a king whose horses drank the water of three oceans.

{{story:ajanta-colours}}

In 1905, Lord Curzon, the British viceroy, announced the [[@bengal-partition-1905|partition of Bengal]]. The province was huge, and the official reason was that it was too big to run well. But many Bengalis believed the real aim was to weaken them by splitting the region along religious lines.

On 7 August 1905, a large meeting at the Calcutta Town Hall agreed to boycott British goods. Soon students were picketing shops that sold foreign goods, standing outside and urging customers to turn away. In some towns, people who kept buying British goods found that neighbours stopped inviting them to weddings and feasts. When the [[partition|partition]] came into force on 16 October, many people fasted and marched. [[@tagore|Rabindranath Tagore]] asked Hindus and Muslims to tie rakhis on each other's wrists as a sign that Bengal was still one.

{{story:tagore}}

India's 2022 tiger count found 785 tigers in Madhya Pradesh, more than in any other state. Many live in national parks such as Kanha, Bandhavgarh, Pench and Satpura. Kanha is also known for saving the barasingha, a swamp deer with many-pointed [[antler|antlers]], from almost vanishing.

These forests may sound familiar. [[@kipling-india|Rudyard Kipling]] set Mowgli's adventures in [[@jungle-book|The Jungle Book]] in the Seoni hills of central India, near today's Pench reserve. Shere Khan the tiger, Baloo the bear and Bagheera the black panther were all based on animals that really live here. Kipling never actually visited Seoni. He built his jungle from the reports and books of people who had.

{{story:jungle-book}}

From 1948, South Africa's government ran a system called [[apartheid|apartheid]], which means "apartness" in Afrikaans. Laws decided where people could live, which schools they could attend, which beaches they could visit and even whom they could marry, all by race. Black South Africans, who were most of the population, could not vote for the national government.

Many people fought this. Some were jailed for years. The most famous was [[@nelson-mandela|Nelson Mandela]], who spent 27 years in prison. Countries around the world refused to play sport with South Africa, and India was one of the first to cut trade ties, in 1946, even before apartheid became law.

{{story:nelson-mandela}}

Myths were told aloud long before they were written. The earliest written versions we have come from two poets of about 700 BCE. Homer is credited with the Iliad, about the war at Troy, and the Odyssey, about one hero's ten-year voyage home. Hesiod wrote the Theogony, which tells how the gods were born.

In Hesiod's version, the world began with Chaos, a yawning emptiness. Then came the earth, Gaia, and [[@myths-giants|a race of giants]] called Titans. Zeus led his brothers and sisters in a war against the Titans and won. Different Greek cities told the stories in different ways, so there was never one official version.

{{story:olympics}}

In the Shatapatha Brahmana, an ancient Sanskrit text, Manu finds a tiny fish while washing his hands. It begs him to protect it from bigger fish. He keeps it in a pot, then a pond, then the sea, as it grows enormous. The fish warns him of a flood and tells him to build a boat.

When the waters rise, Manu ties the boat to the fish's horn, and it pulls him to a northern mountain. In later Puranas, the fish is Matsya, the first avatar of Vishnu. Manu becomes the ancestor of humanity, which is why one Sanskrit word for human, manava, comes from his name.

{{story:mesopotamia}}

Subhadra Kumari Chauhan's Hindi poem "Jhansi ki Rani", written in the 1930s, tells the story of [[@rani-lakshmibai|Rani Lakshmibai]], who fought the British in 1857. Its refrain, "khoob ladi mardani, woh to Jhansi wali rani thi", roughly means "she fought bravely, the queen of Jhansi". Generations of schoolchildren in north India have learned it by heart.

In the United States, [[@langston-hughes|Langston Hughes]] wrote a tiny poem called "Dreams". It asks readers to hold fast to their dreams, because a life without them is "a broken-winged bird that cannot fly".

{{story:poetry}}

Jackfruit trees first grew wild in the [[@rainforests|rainforests]] of the [[@western-ghats|Western Ghats]], along India's southwest coast. People have grown them for thousands of years, and they spread to Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, Southeast Asia and later to Africa, Brazil and Florida.

The English name came through Portuguese traders, who called the fruit "jaca". That word probably came from "chakka", its name in Malayalam. In 2018, [[@kerala|Kerala]] made jackfruit its official state fruit. It is also the national fruit of [[@bangladesh|Bangladesh]], where it is called kathal.

{{story:vegetarian}}

Silk-making began in [[@china-today|China]] at least 5,000 years ago. Chinese legend says that Empress Leizu discovered it when a cocoon fell into her tea and began to unravel. For centuries, China guarded the secret closely, and silk became so valuable that it was traded across Asia to Rome along the [[@silk-road|Silk Road]].

{{story:spiders-silk}}

The [[@byzantium|Byzantine]] historian Procopius wrote that, around the year 550, monks smuggled silkworm eggs out of Asia to the emperor Justinian. Whether every detail is true is unclear, but silk-making did spread west after that.
